<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#baray">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baray</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A baray is an artificial body of water, a common element of the architecture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. The largest are the East Baray and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-baray">West Baray</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> area, each rectangular, oriented east to west and measuring roughly five by one and a half miles. Historians are divided on what barays meant and what they were for. Some hold that their purpose was mainly spiritual, symbolising the Sea of Creation that surrounds Mount Meru, the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> cosmos; others that it was mainly practical, holding water for drinking or for irrigating fields; their function may have combined these or other purposes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baray.jpg" alt="Baray" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Srah_Srang,_Angkor,_Camboya,_2013-08-16,_DD_07.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
